IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `ma-nouns`
IMPORT `ma-reading`
IMPORT `ma-s01-rescission`
IMPORT `ma-s02-damages`
IMPORT `ma-s03-exclusion`
IMPORT `ma-s04-saving`

-- Misrepresentation Act 1967 (Singapore), 2020 Revised Edition, informal consolidation
-- (plain-text rendering of the Singapore Statutes Online text; not the official text) -- THE SEAT LAYER.
--
-- SEAT: a person who entered into a contract after a false statement was made to them, asking on facts
-- they can state whether they may claim damages or rescind, and whether the other party's clause excluding
-- that liability works.  This layer phrases the rules already encoded in the modules imported above as the
-- questions that person would ask.  Nothing here is new law, and no layer-1 module changes to suit it.
--
-- PLAIN INPUTS.  Every question takes plain inputs -- yes/no/not sure, a number, a date, or a choice from a
-- list -- one for each fact the person can state, each with an @desc worded as the person would be asked.
-- The MAYBE inputs the long modules expect are built inside each question from those answers, and the SAME
-- module rule is then called; no rule is restated here.
--   * A fact the person may not know is asked as "Yes / No / I am not sure" (`My answer`); "I am not sure"
--     becomes NOTHING, and a rule that cannot answer without it answers NOTHING.
--   * A date or an amount that the person does not know is asked as 0 and becomes NOTHING.  A day, month and
--     year that do not make a real date roll forward (29 February 2023 is 1 March 2023).
--   * The Act defines no term, so "misrepresentation", "rescind", "fraudulently", "loss", "equitable" and
--     "reasonableness" (section 11(1) of the Unfair Contract Terms Act 1977, not among the sources) are
--     asked as facts: the person states them, or says they are not sure.
--
-- HOW TO READ A RESULT.  JUST TRUE and JUST FALSE are answers the Act itself gives; NOTHING means the Act
-- does not answer on these facts (see NOTES.md, "Reading a result: FALSE and NOTHING").  Never read NOTHING as no.
-- Where the Act lets the court or an arbitrator decide ("may declare", "if of opinion"), the answer says what
-- it MAY do, not what it WILL do.
--
-- A question is asked of the contracting person's own situation.  There is no seat for the court or an
-- arbitrator (s 2(2)): the power the Act gives them is met only through the questions below.  Section 5 (the
-- short title) creates no rule and has no question.

DECLARE `My answer` IS ONE OF
    Yes
    No
    `I am not sure`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Wiring: the person's answers become the modules' inputs.  Not questions.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- An answer of Yes, No or "I am not sure" as a MAYBE BOOLEAN.
GIVEN answer IS A `My answer`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`what I say, as a fact` MEANS
    CONSIDER answer
    WHEN Yes THEN JUST TRUE -- input mapping
    WHEN No THEN JUST FALSE -- input mapping
    WHEN `I am not sure` THEN NOTHING -- input mapping

-- A date from what the person knows: 0 in any part means not known.
GIVEN year IS A NUMBER
      month IS A NUMBER
      day IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`a date from what I know` MEANS
    IF year EQUALS 0 OR month EQUALS 0 OR day EQUALS 0
    THEN NOTHING -- input mapping
    ELSE JUST (Date day month year) -- input mapping

-- An amount that the person gives as 0 when they do not know it.
GIVEN amount IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`my amount if I know it` MEANS
    IF amount EQUALS 0 THEN NOTHING ELSE JUST amount -- input mapping

§ `The Misrepresentation Act 1967 -- my questions`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
§§ `Can I rescind the contract? (s 1)`
-- Situation: asked only of a person who entered into a contract after a misrepresentation was made to them.
-- The form can ask "Was a false statement made to you before you signed the contract?" before showing this group.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- s 1.  Where a person has entered into a contract after a misrepresentation has been made to him, and the
-- misrepresentation has become a term of the contract or the contract has been performed (or both), then, if
-- otherwise he would be entitled to rescind the contract without alleging fraud, he is so entitled,
-- notwithstanding those two matters.  The section is "subject to the provisions of this Act": the other
-- sections are the questions below, each answered on its own.
@export Am I entitled to rescind the contract even though the false statement became a term of it or the contract has been performed (s 1)?
GIVEN `I entered into the contract after a misrepresentation was made to me` IS A `My answer` @desc Did you enter into the contract after a misrepresentation (a false statement of fact) was made to you?
      `the statement became a term of the contract` IS A `My answer` @desc Has the statement become a term of the contract?
      `the contract has been performed` IS A `My answer` @desc Has the contract been performed?
      `I would otherwise be entitled to rescind` IS A `My answer` @desc Leaving those two matters aside, would you be entitled to rescind the contract because of the statement without alleging fraud?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Am I entitled to rescind despite the term or the performance` IS
    `s 1 the person is entitled to rescind notwithstanding the matters mentioned in paragraphs (a) and (b)` (`what I say, as a fact` `I entered into the contract after a misrepresentation was made to me`) (`what I say, as a fact` `the statement became a term of the contract`) (`what I say, as a fact` `the contract has been performed`) (`what I say, as a fact` `I would otherwise be entitled to rescind`)

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
§§ `Can I claim damages for the false statement? (s 2)`
-- Situation: asked only of a person who entered into a contract after a misrepresentation was made to them by another party to the contract.
-- The form can ask "Did the other party to the contract make a false statement to you before you signed?" before showing this group.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- s 2(1).  Where a person has entered into a contract after a misrepresentation has been made to him by another
-- party to it and as a result he has suffered loss, then, if the person making it would be liable to damages had
-- it been made fraudulently, that person is liable although it was not made fraudulently, unless he proves that
-- he had reasonable ground to believe, and did believe up to the time the contract was made, that the facts
-- represented were true.
@export Is the other party liable to pay me damages even though the false statement was not made fraudulently (s 2(1))?
GIVEN `I entered into the contract after a misrepresentation was made to me by another party to it` IS A `My answer` @desc Did you enter into the contract after a misrepresentation (a false statement of fact) was made to you by another party to the contract?
      `I suffered loss as a result` IS A `My answer` @desc Did you suffer loss as a result of the misrepresentation?
      `they would be liable in damages if it had been fraudulent` IS A `My answer` @desc If the misrepresentation had been made fraudulently, would the person who made it have been liable to you in damages?
      `they have proved reasonable ground to believe` IS A `My answer` @desc Has the person who made the statement proved that they had reasonable ground to believe that the facts they stated were true?
      `they have proved belief up to the time of the contract` IS A `My answer` @desc Has the person who made the statement proved that they did believe, up to the time the contract was made, that the facts they stated were true?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is the other party liable to pay me damages` IS
    `s 2(1) the person making the misrepresentation is liable to damages` (`what I say, as a fact` `I entered into the contract after a misrepresentation was made to me by another party to it`) (`what I say, as a fact` `I suffered loss as a result`) (`what I say, as a fact` `they would be liable in damages if it had been fraudulent`) (`what I say, as a fact` `they have proved reasonable ground to believe`) (`what I say, as a fact` `they have proved belief up to the time of the contract`)

-- s 2(1), the end of the sentence.  "Unless he proves ...": if the other party has not proved both matters,
-- this section gives them no defence on these facts (it does not say they have no defence at all).
@export Does section 2(1) give the other party a defence because they proved reasonable ground to believe and belief up to the time of the contract (s 2(1))?
GIVEN `they have proved reasonable ground to believe` IS A `My answer` @desc Has the person who made the statement proved that they had reasonable ground to believe that the facts they stated were true?
      `they have proved belief up to the time of the contract` IS A `My answer` @desc Has the person who made the statement proved that they did believe, up to the time the contract was made, that the facts they stated were true?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Does section 2(1) give the other party a defence` IS
    `s 2(1) he proves reasonable ground to believe and belief up to the time the contract was made` (`what I say, as a fact` `they have proved reasonable ground to believe`) (`what I say, as a fact` `they have proved belief up to the time of the contract`)

-- s 2(2).  Where I entered into the contract after a misrepresentation made otherwise than fraudulently and I
-- would be entitled to rescind, then, if it is claimed in proceedings that the contract ought to be or has been
-- rescinded, the court or arbitrator may declare the contract subsisting and award damages instead of
-- rescission, if of opinion that it would be equitable, having regard to three matters.
-- A misrepresentation made fraudulently, or no entitlement to rescind, fails the same "Where" clause: the answer is No.
@export May the court or arbitrator keep the contract going and award me damages instead of letting me rescind (s 2(2))?
GIVEN `I entered into the contract after a misrepresentation was made to me` IS A `My answer` @desc Did you enter into the contract after a misrepresentation (a false statement of fact) was made to you?
      `it was made otherwise than fraudulently` IS A `My answer` @desc Was the misrepresentation made otherwise than fraudulently? (Answer No if it was made fraudulently.)
      `I would be entitled to rescind because of it` IS A `My answer` @desc Would you be entitled, because of the misrepresentation, to rescind the contract?
      `rescission is claimed in proceedings` IS A `My answer` @desc Is it claimed, in proceedings arising out of the contract, that the contract ought to be or has been rescinded?
      `who is deciding` IS A `Who is deciding` @desc Who is deciding the claim? Choose one of: The court / An arbitrator / Another body
      `it thinks it would be equitable` IS A `My answer` @desc Is the court or arbitrator of the opinion that it would be equitable to declare the contract subsisting and award damages instead of rescission?
      `it had regard to the nature of the misrepresentation` IS A `My answer` @desc Did the court or arbitrator form that opinion having regard to the nature of the misrepresentation?
      `it had regard to the loss if the contract is upheld` IS A `My answer` @desc Did it have regard to the loss that the misrepresentation would cause you if the contract were upheld?
      `it had regard to the loss rescission would cause the other party` IS A `My answer` @desc Did it have regard to the loss that rescission would cause to the other party?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May the court or arbitrator award damages instead of rescission` IS
    `s 2(2) the court or arbitrator may declare the contract subsisting and award damages in lieu of rescission` (`what I say, as a fact` `I entered into the contract after a misrepresentation was made to me`) (`what I say, as a fact` `it was made otherwise than fraudulently`) (`what I say, as a fact` `I would be entitled to rescind because of it`) (`what I say, as a fact` `rescission is claimed in proceedings`) `who is deciding` (`what I say, as a fact` `it thinks it would be equitable`) (`what I say, as a fact` `it had regard to the nature of the misrepresentation`) (`what I say, as a fact` `it had regard to the loss if the contract is upheld`) (`what I say, as a fact` `it had regard to the loss rescission would cause the other party`)

-- s 2(3).  Damages may be awarded under s 2(2) whether or not the other party is liable to me under s 2(1).
-- The answer under s 2(2) is what you were told (the question just above); the answer under s 2(1) makes no difference.
@export Can damages be awarded instead of rescission even if the other party is not liable to me under section 2(1) (s 2(3))?
GIVEN `damages instead of rescission are available under section 2(2)` IS A `My answer` @desc Is it the case that the court or arbitrator may declare the contract subsisting and award you damages instead of rescission under section 2(2)?
      `the other party is liable to me under section 2(1)` IS A `My answer` @desc Is the other party liable to you in damages under section 2(1)?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Can damages be awarded instead of rescission whether or not liable under section 2(1)` IS
    `s 2(3) damages may be awarded under subsection (2) whether or not he is liable to damages under subsection (1)` (`what I say, as a fact` `damages instead of rescission are available under section 2(2)`) (`what I say, as a fact` `the other party is liable to me under section 2(1)`)

-- s 2(3).  Where the other party is liable to me under s 2(1), any award under s 2(2) is taken into account in
-- assessing that liability.
@export If damages are awarded instead of rescission, must they be taken into account in working out the other party's liability to me (s 2(3))?
GIVEN `the other party is liable to me under section 2(1)` IS A `My answer` @desc Is the other party liable to you in damages under section 2(1)?
      `an award has been made under section 2(2)` IS A `My answer` @desc Has an award of damages instead of rescission been made under section 2(2)?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must the award be taken into account` IS
    `s 2(3) an award under subsection (2) is to be taken into account in assessing his liability under subsection (1)` (`what I say, as a fact` `the other party is liable to me under section 2(1)`) (`what I say, as a fact` `an award has been made under section 2(2)`)

-- s 2(3).  How much is to be taken into account: the award itself.  The Act says "taken into account", not
-- "deducted", so no net figure is worked out here.
@export If the court or arbitrator has awarded me damages instead of letting me rescind, how much of that award is to be taken into account when working out what the other party owes me, in dollars (s 2(3))?
GIVEN `the other party is liable to me under section 2(1)` IS A `My answer` @desc Is the other party liable to pay you damages for the false statement under section 2(1)?
      `an award has been made under section 2(2)` IS A `My answer` @desc Has the court or arbitrator already awarded you damages instead of letting you rescind (section 2(2))?
      `the amount of the award` IS A NUMBER @desc How much money, in dollars, did the court or arbitrator award you instead of letting you rescind? (0 if you do not know)
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
DECIDE `How much of the award is to be taken into account` IS
    `s 2(3) the amount of the award under subsection (2) that is to be taken into account` (`what I say, as a fact` `the other party is liable to me under section 2(1)`) (`what I say, as a fact` `an award has been made under section 2(2)`) (`my amount if I know it` `the amount of the award`)

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
§§ `Does the other party's clause excluding that liability work? (s 3)`
-- Situation: asked only of a person whose contract contains a term that excludes or restricts a liability or remedy for a misrepresentation.
-- The form can ask "Does the contract say the other party is not liable for what it told you before the contract, or that you cannot use a remedy for it?" before showing this group.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- s 3.  If a contract contains a term which would exclude or restrict a party's liability for a misrepresentation
-- made by that party before the contract was made, or another party's remedy for such a misrepresentation, that
-- term is of no effect except in so far as it satisfies the requirement of reasonableness stated in section 11(1)
-- of the Unfair Contract Terms Act 1977.  Ask it of the whole clause, or of the part of it you are asking about.
@export Is the other party's clause excluding or restricting their liability, or my remedy, for the false statement of no effect (s 3)?
GIVEN `the clause excludes or restricts the other party's liability for a statement made before the contract` IS A `My answer` @desc Does the clause (or the part of it you are asking about) exclude or restrict a liability of a party to the contract for a misrepresentation that party made before the contract was made?
      `the clause excludes or restricts my remedy for such a statement` IS A `My answer` @desc Does the clause (or the part) exclude or restrict a remedy that a party to the contract has because of such a misrepresentation?
      `the clause satisfies the requirement of reasonableness` IS A `My answer` @desc Does the clause (or the part) satisfy the requirement of reasonableness as stated in section 11(1) of the Unfair Contract Terms Act 1977?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is the clause of no effect` IS
    `s 3 the term is of no effect` (`what I say, as a fact` `the clause excludes or restricts the other party's liability for a statement made before the contract`) (`what I say, as a fact` `the clause excludes or restricts my remedy for such a statement`) (`what I say, as a fact` `the clause satisfies the requirement of reasonableness`)

-- s 3, the whole sentence read together.  The question above takes reasonableness as a fact you state.  This one takes it as what
-- has been shown: the Act says it is for those claiming that the term satisfies the requirement to show that it does, so a clause
-- within (a) or (b) that they have not shown to satisfy it is of no effect.  "Not sure" whether it has been shown gives NOTHING.
@export If the other party who relies on the clause has not shown that it is reasonable, is the clause of no effect (s 3)?
GIVEN `the clause excludes or restricts the other party's liability for a statement made before the contract` IS A `My answer` @desc Does the clause (or the part of it you are asking about) exclude or restrict a liability of a party to the contract for a misrepresentation that party made before the contract was made?
      `the clause excludes or restricts my remedy for such a statement` IS A `My answer` @desc Does the clause (or the part) exclude or restrict a remedy that a party to the contract has because of such a misrepresentation?
      `the other party has shown that the clause satisfies the requirement` IS A `My answer` @desc Has the other party, who is claiming that the clause is reasonable, shown that the clause (or the part) satisfies the requirement of reasonableness as stated in section 11(1) of the Unfair Contract Terms Act 1977?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is the clause of no effect given that the other party must show it is reasonable` IS
    `s 3 the term is of no effect, it being for those claiming that it satisfies the requirement to show that it does` (`what I say, as a fact` `the clause excludes or restricts the other party's liability for a statement made before the contract`) (`what I say, as a fact` `the clause excludes or restricts my remedy for such a statement`) (`what I say, as a fact` `the other party has shown that the clause satisfies the requirement`)

-- s 3, the end of the sentence.  It is for those claiming that the term satisfies the requirement to show that it does.
@export Is it for the other party, who relies on the clause, to show that the clause satisfies the requirement of reasonableness (s 3)?
GIVEN `the clause excludes or restricts the other party's liability for a statement made before the contract` IS A `My answer` @desc Does the clause (or the part of it you are asking about) exclude or restrict a liability of a party to the contract for a misrepresentation that party made before the contract was made?
      `the clause excludes or restricts my remedy for such a statement` IS A `My answer` @desc Does the clause (or the part) exclude or restrict a remedy that a party to the contract has because of such a misrepresentation?
      `the other party claims that the clause satisfies the requirement` IS A `My answer` @desc Is the other party claiming that the clause satisfies the requirement of reasonableness?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is it for the other party to show the clause is reasonable` IS
    `s 3 it is for those claiming that the term satisfies that requirement to show that it does` (`what I say, as a fact` `the clause excludes or restricts the other party's liability for a statement made before the contract`) (`what I say, as a fact` `the clause excludes or restricts my remedy for such a statement`) (`what I say, as a fact` `the other party claims that the clause satisfies the requirement`)

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
§§ `Does the Act apply to a statement or contract from long ago? (s 4)`
-- Situation: asked only of a person whose false statement or contract may date from before 12 November 1993.
-- The form can ask "Was the false statement made, or the contract of sale entered into, before 12 November 1993?" before showing this group.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- s 4.  Nothing in the Act applies to a misrepresentation or contract of sale made before 12 November 1993,
-- except in so far as the Act was applicable by virtue of section 5 of the Civil Law Act 1909 in force before
-- that date.  For a statement made on or after 12 November 1993 (and a contract of sale made on or after it) the section does not
-- bar the Act, and the answer is No; the day 12 November 1993 itself is not "before" it.
@export Does section 4 stop this Act applying to the false statement or to my contract of sale (s 4)?
GIVEN `the year the statement was made` IS A NUMBER @desc In what year was the false statement made to you? (0 if you do not know)
      `the month the statement was made` IS A NUMBER @desc In which month was it made, from 1 for January to 12 for December? (0 if you do not know)
      `the day the statement was made` IS A NUMBER @desc On which day of the month was it made? (0 if you do not know)
      `my contract is a contract of sale` IS A `My answer` @desc Is your contract a contract of sale?
      `the year the contract was made` IS A NUMBER @desc In what year was the contract made? (0 if you do not know)
      `the month the contract was made` IS A NUMBER @desc In which month was it made, from 1 for January to 12 for December? (0 if you do not know)
      `the day the contract was made` IS A NUMBER @desc On which day of the month was it made? (0 if you do not know)
      `the Act was applicable under the Civil Law Act 1909` IS A `My answer` @desc Was this Act applicable to the statement or contract by virtue of section 5 of the Civil Law Act 1909 as in force before 12 November 1993?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Does section 4 stop the Act applying` IS
    `s 4 nothing in this Act applies in relation to the misrepresentation or contract of sale` (`a date from what I know` `the year the statement was made` `the month the statement was made` `the day the statement was made`) (`what I say, as a fact` `my contract is a contract of sale`) (`a date from what I know` `the year the contract was made` `the month the contract was made` `the day the contract was made`) (`what I say, as a fact` `the Act was applicable under the Civil Law Act 1909`)

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
§§ `Whom and what do the Act's words cover? (Interpretation Act 1965 s 2(1))`
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- The Act speaks of "a person" and "a party".  The Interpretation Act 1965 s 2(1) says "person" and "party"
-- include a company, an association and a body of persons.  An individual is not named there.
@export Does the Act's word "person" or "party" include me or the other party (Interpretation Act s 2(1))?
GIVEN `what kind of party` IS A `Kind of party` @desc What kind of party is it? Choose one of: An individual / A company / An association / Another body of persons / Something else
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Does the Act's word person or party include this party` IS
    `ss 1 to 3 "person" and "party" include the party (Interpretation Act s 2(1))` `what kind of party`

-- Section 2(2) speaks of "the court".  The Interpretation Act 1965 s 2(1) says "court" means any court of
-- competent jurisdiction in Singapore.
@export Does the Act count the body that is deciding my claim as a "court" (s 2(2))?
GIVEN `what body is deciding` IS A `Body asked about` @desc What is the body that is deciding your claim? Choose one of: A court of competent jurisdiction in Singapore / Any other court / Any other body
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is the body deciding my claim a court` IS
    `s 2(2) the body is a "court" (Interpretation Act s 2(1))` `what body is deciding`
